+## Private Cache and Eviction
 
 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Cache Management
+
+The cache uses the Two-Queue (2Q) algorithm to balance protection of reused sheets against sequential screening workloads. Entries flow through two logical queues with independent eviction behavior.
+
+**Queue A** holds new entries requested once. Entries in A are subject to FIFO eviction when the cache reaches capacity. The size of A is capped at 25% of the total cache capacity (3 GiB). When A reaches this size, further new entries are admitted by evicting the oldest entry in A.
+
+**Queue B** holds entries that have been accessed a second time. Entries in B use LRU eviction — the least recently used entry in B is evicted when needed. Queue B expands to fill remaining capacity (up to 9 GiB). The separation ensures that one-time screenings do not evict heavily reused comparison sheets.
+
+**Promotion mechanism**: When an entry in A receives a second access, it moves to B and is timestamp-updated. Once in B, subsequent accesses update its LRU timestamp but never move it back to A. Entries in B remain until evicted by LRU pressure or explicit (e.g., operator-triggered) clearing.
+
+This choice trades two separate queues and a size-split parameter for protection of repeated-use entries during large sequential screenings — a workload mix the preview worker observes in practice.
